# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for WalPE's test runs (TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=<this file>;
# `meson test --setup=tsan` passes it). One suppression per line, `kind:pattern`, where the
# pattern is matched against the function, file and library names of a report's stack:
#   race:<pattern>            a data race report with a matching frame
#   race_top:<pattern>        ... only when the top frame matches
#   thread:<pattern>          a thread leak
#   mutex:<pattern>           a destroyed locked mutex and the like
#   deadlock:<pattern>        a lock-order inversion
#   signal:<pattern>          a signal-unsafe call in a handler
#   called_from_lib:<lib.so>  ignore everything intercepted while inside that library
#
# This is the one suppression file of the tree: the test setup `tsan` reads it, every other
# thread-sanitizer run of the tree should too, and no other file is read.
#
# It is empty on purpose: every report is looked at first. A line is added only for
# a report that is not this program's to fix (a library built without the sanitizer that
# locks with a primitive the sanitizer cannot see), with the reason on the line above it,
# so that a real race is never hidden by a pattern that happens to match. The rule for a
# new line, all of it:
#   - a real run printed the report (never a guess at what might turn up);
#   - both stacks lie in a library that is not built with the sanitizer, or one does and
#     the same report appears without any code of this tree involved (the
#     race-shim test is the place to try that);
#   - the line names the narrowest thing that silences it (one function of that library,
#     or called_from_lib:<its file>), with the first lines of the report and the date as
#     a comment above it.
# A report with code of this tree on both sides is never suppressed: it is fixed, or
# written down as open. Libraries known
# for such reports, should they turn up: GLib's bit locks and one-time initialisation
# (libglib-2.0, libgobject-2.0), Mesa's software rasteriser threads (libgallium, swrast_dri;
# the test setup already runs it single-threaded with LP_NUM_THREADS=0), fontconfig's
# cache objects, FFmpeg's frame threads.
